<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:id="@+id/coordinatorLayout"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:fitsSystemWindows="true">

    <!-- ⚠️ NON-COLLAPSING BY DESIGN (2026-09-05). Do not reintroduce a CollapsingToolbarLayout here.
         WHY: the fast-scroll rail is drawn into the RecyclerView's ViewGroupOverlay (FastScroller's
         constructor calls mView.getOverlay().add(...) three times), so the rail can only ever span the
         RecyclerView. Under a CollapsingToolbarLayout, HeaderScrollingViewBehavior sized the RecyclerView
         to `viewport - collapsedHeight` and positioned it BELOW the header, so the rail began ~633px down
         the screen and the thumb rested there at the top of its own track. Four models were built hunting
         a displaced thumb before a rest: capture showed offset=0 padTop=0 thumbFrac=0.000 — it had always
         been at the top of a track that started too low. Drawing the rail higher dies on touch delivery:
         a RecyclerView.OnItemTouchListener only sees events delivered to the RecyclerView.
         With no `scroll` flag on any child, AppBarLayout.getTotalScrollRange() breaks its loop at i=0 and
         returns Math.max(0, 0) = 0 (decoded from the 1.14.0 AAR), so PixelFastScrollViewHelper's composite
         terms are both 0 and the arithmetic reduces to the flat-screen path exactly — the same reduction
         Home already gets. The cover moved into MediaHeaderAdapter (item 0); the
         TITLE did not follow it there - it is set on the toolbar instead (MediaFragment,
         binding.toolBar.title).
         DELIBERATELY GIVEN UP: the pinned collapsed title and the cover parallax/fade.

         paddingBottom="49dp" IS NOT MISSING — it was DELETED, not lost. It was residue of the CTL's
         circular measurement dependency (AppBarLayout and CTL both wrap_content, CTL height scaling ~1.5x
         paddingBottom rather than 1:1, cover size and expanded-title space contending through one knob),
         settled empirically at 43dp on 2026-06-15 after three uiautomator dumps and later carried at 49dp.
         With the CTL gone none of those inputs exist and the value has no justification HERE.
         (!) BUT IT WAS DOING A SECOND JOB, AND THIS NOTE ORIGINALLY MISSED IT (corrected 2026-09-06): the
         padding sat below the cover, so its visual effect was the cover-to-buttons gap, which is a design
         element and outlived the CTL. Deleting it flattened that gap to 0 and was reported from device as
         a regression on artist, album and playlist. The gap is now rebuilt where it belongs - as
         paddingBottom on the cover wrapper in item_media_header.xml, at 49dp + the RecyclerView's 8dp
         paddingTop = 57dp, since that 8dp now falls above the cover instead of below it. Read the note
         there before touching either number. What stays true is the narrow claim: do not restore 49dp TO
         THIS FILE, where nothing measures against it any more. The same applies to the 2026-06-13 title work — titleCollapseMode fade,
         maxLines 2, expandedTitleMarginEnd, and the expandedTitleEllipsize attribute that does not exist in
         Material 1.14.0 — all CTL attributes with no successor here. -->
    <!-- ⚠️ DECLARED FIRST, ON PURPOSE (2026-09-06). Z-ORDER IS THE WHOLE REASON. With the overlap
         behavior below, this view's bounds now COVER the toolbar region, and a CoordinatorLayout
         hit-tests its children in reverse declaration order — so declared last, as it was until now, it
         would swallow every touch aimed at the back button and the overflow menu. Moving it first makes
         the AppBarLayout the later sibling: drawn on top, touched first.
         CHOSEN OVER elevation/translationZ deliberately. Both work, but a Z override competes with
         Material's own elevation and lift-on-scroll machinery and with appbarOutline's translationZ=1dp,
         and it is invisible when reading the file. Declaration order is static, local and obvious.
         Layout is unaffected by the move: ScrollingViewBehavior.layoutDependsOn finds the AppBarLayout by
         type, and CoordinatorLayout measures in dependency order regardless of declaration order.
         (appbarOutline still comes AFTER the AppBarLayout — layout_anchor requires its anchor to be
         declared before it.)

         ⚠️ WHAT THIS DOES TO D-PAD FOCUS ON TV, since there is no values-television variant of this file
         and artist/album/playlist detail get this layout on TV too. Answered from the android-34 framework
         sources, not from device:
           DIRECTIONAL D-PAD (UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT) — UNAFFECTED. FocusFinder.findNextFocus routes those to
             findNextFocusInAbsoluteDirection (FocusFinder.java:264-270), which is geometric. The toolbar is
             still physically above the list, so UP from the top row still reaches it.
           FOCUS_FORWARD/BACKWARD — ALSO EFFECTIVELY UNAFFECTED. It walks the focusables list, but
             ViewGroup.addFocusables sorts that list with FocusFinder.sort (ViewGroup.java:1341), whose
             comparator orders by rect.top then sides (FocusFinder.java:792-822). Geometry again, not
             declaration order.
           INITIAL FOCUS — THIS ONE DOES CHANGE, and it is the only one that does.
             ViewGroup.onRequestFocusInDescendants (ViewGroup.java:3355-3380) iterates mChildren in RAW
             INDEX ORDER with no sort. The AppBarLayout used to be child 0, so first focus landed on the
             toolbar's navigation icon; the FragmentContainerView is child 0 now, so first focus lands in
             the RecyclerView — on item 0's buttons. That is the behaviour MediaFragment's own note (at the
             removed `if (isTV) setExpanded(false, false)`) says it wants: "the first D-pad focus lands on a
             button". Before this reorder that note's premise was actually false.
           IS THE TOOLBAR FOCUSABLE ON TV? The MaterialToolbar ViewGroup is not, but the views it inflates
             are — the navigation icon is an ImageButton and menu items are ActionMenuItemViews, both
             focusable by default, and @style/Toolbar sets no focusability attributes. So it is D-pad
             reachable before and after; only the ORDER of the first landing changed.
           ⚠️ AND THE RecyclerView paddingTop IS LOAD-BEARING FOR TV, not just cosmetic: the list now spans
             under the toolbar, so a focused row could sit beneath it. requestChildRectangleOnScreen
             scrolls a focused child into the PADDED content area, and paddingTop = insets.top +
             ?actionBarSize is what keeps that area clear of the toolbar. (Inference: LinearLayoutManager
             honours padding when computing that scroll — not decoded here.) -->
    <androidx.fragment.app.FragmentContainerView
        android:id="@+id/mediaFragmentContainer"
        android:name="dev.brahmkshatriya.echo.ui.media.MediaDetailsFragment"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        app:layout_behavior="dev.brahmkshatriya.echo.utils.ui.OverlapScrollingViewBehavior"
        tools:layout="@layout/fragment_media_details" />

    <!-- ⚠️ THIS SCRIM IS NEW UI, NOT A RESTORATION — stated because the opposite is easy to assume. No
         contentScrim was ever set on this screen: @style/EchoCollapsingBar never had one, and the only
         contentScrim line in the project is a COMMENTED-OUT item in @style/TopCollapsingBar (styles.xml).
         The multi-line title overlapping the cover during CTL expansion was recorded at the time as
         standard Material 3 CTL behaviour with no contentScrim set. So nothing was lost when the CTL went;
         this gradient is a new element, introduced because the art now scrolls under the toolbar.

         ⚠️ NOT THE TV-PLAYER BUG, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. An earlier session found the TV
         player's cover and MaterialToolbar sharing the same top strip because a layout_marginTop had been
         trimmed from ?actionBarSize to 24dp while the cover stayed constrained to parent top; the fix was
         to restore ?actionBarSize and accept a ~32dp cover give-back. That overlap was ACCIDENTAL and
         UNSCRIMMED — two opaque things colliding with nothing between them. This one is DELIBERATE,
         SCRIMMED, and paired with RecyclerView paddingTop = insets.top + ?actionBarSize (see
         MediaDetailsFragment), so content STARTS below the toolbar and only travels under it while
         scrolling. Different screen, different mechanism; do not "fix" this one by restoring a margin.

         ⚠️ BACKGROUND IS A SCRIM, NOT A COLOUR, and that is the whole readability answer. Material's
         default (Widget.Material3.AppBarLayout via ?appBarLayoutStyle) paints ?colorSurface here, which
         would sit as an opaque band over the album art now that the art scrolls underneath. A flat
         transparent background would leave the back arrow and overflow icons unreadable on light artwork.
         The gradient does both jobs in one attribute: opaque-ish at the status bar, fully transparent at
         the toolbar's bottom edge, over exactly the region this view occupies (status-bar inset via
         fitsSystemWindows + ?actionBarSize).
         FALLBACK IF IT STILL READS BADLY ON LIGHT ART, recorded so it is a decision rather than a
         rediscovery: per-icon scrims — small translucent circles behind the navigation and overflow icons
         only, which is what YouTube Music does on its artist header. Not chosen first because it needs a
         drawable and a style override per icon, and because it does not help the toolbar TITLE, which is
         also drawn over the art here. The gradient is one attribute and covers all three.
         liftOnScroll=false + stateListAnimator=null: without them, AppBarLayout raises elevation and
         paints a surface tint as soon as the list scrolls, which puts the opaque band back the moment the
         user moves. Setting liftOnScroll=false also stops BaseBehavior.onStartNestedScroll accepting the
         scroll at all — decoded from the 1.14.0 AAR, it returns true only when lift-on-scroll is on, the
         bar is already lifted, or the bar has scrollable children, and with no scroll flags there are
         none. It consumed no scroll pixels before (totalScrollRange is 0), so nothing downstream of
         PixelFastScrollViewHelper changes. -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout
        android:id="@+id/appBarLayout"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:background="@drawable/bg_toolbar_scrim"
        android:fitsSystemWindows="true"
        android:stateListAnimator="@null"
        app:liftOnScroll="false">

        <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
            android:id="@+id/toolBar"
            style="@style/Toolbar"
            android:background="@android:color/transparent"
            app:menu="@menu/more_menu"
            tools:title="@tools:sample/full_names" />

    </com.google.android.material.appbar.AppBarLayout>

    <View
        android:id="@+id/appbarOutline"
        style="@style/AppBarOutline"
        app:layout_anchor="@id/appBarLayout"
        app:layout_anchorGravity="bottom" />

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>